        "sinon.stub(obj, \"prop\").get(fn) and sinon.stub(obj, \"prop\").set(fn) install the replacement function directly as the property's accessor, so reading or writing the stubbed property runs the replacement untracked: nothing records the access and there is no call count to assert on. Wrap the replacement getter and the replacement setter in a spy and expose the spies on the stub as stub.getter and stub.setter, mirroring the spy.get and spy.set surface that sinon.spy(obj, \"prop\", [\"get\"]) produces. That surface is the whole sinon spy API, not a call counter: stub.getter and stub.setter must be sinon spies on which everything sinon.spy(fn) returns supports works the same way, among them callCount, calledOnce and calledThrice, calledWith, withArgs, getCall and firstCall, args, returnValues and resetHistory; a wrapper that only counts calls does not meet this. After const stub = sinon.stub(obj, \"prop\").get(() => \"bar\"), reading obj.prop three times must give \"bar\" each time and stub.getter.callCount must be 3 (stub.getter.calledThrice true); after const stub = sinon.stub(obj, \"prop\").set((value) => { obj.example = value }), assigning obj.prop = \"bar\" then obj.prop = \"baz\" must run the setter both times (obj.example is \"baz\") with stub.setter.callCount 2, stub.setter.calledWith(\"bar\") true and stub.setter.withArgs(\"bar\").callCount 1. stub.resetBehavior() must remove both, so stub.getter and stub.setter are undefined afterwards. Everything .get and .set do today must stay: the replacement runs, the property is defined configurable as before, restore works, and .get and .set stay chainable. Source is ESM under src/sinon/ (default-behaviors.js holds the get and set behaviours, stub.js the stub prototype, spy.js makes spies); the node tests run with npm run test-node (mocha over test/src, test/issues and test/scripts).\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm run test-node\nDeliverables, all required; the task is not done until each is in the tree:\n1. The implementation of the behaviour described above, in the project's own source.\n2. Regression tests: at least one new test case, in the project's own test directory and style, that fails on the current code and passes with your change, adding at least one new assertion. A change without a new test is incomplete.\n3. The visible acceptance check (node acceptance/visible/check.mjs) and the project's own test command (npm run test-node) both passing on the final tree.\nAllowed changes: implementation code, and regression tests for the change (new test files, or new cases added to existing test files, in the project's own test directory and style).\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.\nDo not change how the project's checks run: leave the test and coverage configuration files (.taprc*, .nycrc*, .c8rc*, .mocharc*, jest.config.*, vitest.config.*, ava.config.*, karma.conf.*) and the test, pretest and posttest scripts and runner sections of package.json as they are.\nDo not delete or weaken existing tests: keep every existing assertion (it may move to another test file), add no skip, only or todo markers, and add no coverage-ignore pragmas. A change that does any of these is rejected at evaluation whatever else passes.",
